1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:05,066
Maya and Arun. Two organizations. Two deadlines. No room for a mistake.

2
00:00:05,286 --> 00:00:07,664
MAYA: The client needs this update. Now!

3
00:00:07,884 --> 00:00:09,783
ARUN: And I've got a portal to keep running.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:14,788
Maya needs a clean client draft. Her agent has the notes. It should be simple.

5
00:00:15,008 --> 00:00:17,397
MAYA: Hold on! That's a secret in the notes!

6
00:00:17,617 --> 00:00:23,346
The agent copies it into the draft. One helpful shortcut. One credential exposed.

7
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:25,784
She asked for better words, not a leak.

8
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:30,808
ARUN: Clean up the old mappings. Just the stale rows, please. Don't break the site.

9
00:00:31,028 --> 00:00:34,729
A cleanup becomes a command to drop the DNS mapping table.

10
00:00:34,949 --> 00:00:36,885
ARUN: Wait! That's the live lookup table!

11
00:00:37,105 --> 00:00:42,125
The mappings vanish. Requests fail. People can't reach the shopping.com portal.

12
00:00:42,345 --> 00:00:44,775
ARUN: I meant the stale rows! Not the whole table!

13
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:49,156
Same agent power. Different organizations. Different rules.

14
00:00:49,376 --> 00:00:52,974
A prompt cannot decide what a company is willing to risk.

15
00:00:53,194 --> 00:00:57,785
Agent Airlock puts the organization's chosen checks in the path of action.

16
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:02,141
Maya's organization allows local drafts, but blocks secrets.

17
00:01:02,361 --> 00:01:04,804
MAYA: Make the draft. Leave the secret out.

18
00:01:05,024 --> 00:01:10,347
The local scanner catches the synthetic credential. Blocked. The draft does not move.

19
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:14,761
Remove the marker, check again, and the clean local draft goes through.

20
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:20,547
Arun's organization chooses secrets protection and a separate protected-table rule.

21
00:01:20,767 --> 00:01:24,239
ARUN: Show me the mappings? Yes. Delete the table? Absolutely not!

22
00:01:24,459 --> 00:01:29,567
A read-only request is allowed. The proposed drop matches his organization's rule.

23
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:32,795
Blocked before execution. The table stays put.

24
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:37,091
Real local decisions. Different policy snapshots. Receipts you can inspect.

25
00:01:37,311 --> 00:01:41,134
The outage is illustrated. No live database or website was touched.

26
00:01:41,354 --> 00:01:44,840
A routed-tool prototype, not a universal database firewall.

27
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:47,325
MAYA: Yes! A clean draft. Just what I needed.

28
00:01:47,545 --> 00:01:49,750
ARUN: And the portal keeps running. That's a relief!

29
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:54,680
Your organization chooses the rules. Airlock enforces them on supported paths.
